WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:10.143
The Earth may not be our home forever.

00:00:10.143 --> 00:00:12.979
Eventually, we may have to leave.

00:00:12.979 --> 00:00:16.649
What if instead of finding a
potentially habitable exoplanet

00:00:16.649 --> 00:00:18.051
light-years away,

00:00:18.051 --> 00:00:19.753
we stayed in our Solar System 

00:00:19.753 --> 00:00:21.888
and built a habitat so enormous

00:00:21.888 --> 00:00:24.924
we could never overpopulate it?

00:00:24.924 --> 00:00:26.526
This is WHAT IF,

00:00:26.526 --> 00:00:27.794
and here's what would happen

00:00:27.794 --> 00:00:30.163
if we built a ringworld in space.

00:00:31.998 --> 00:00:34.067
Imagine you lived on a ring

00:00:34.067 --> 00:00:37.570
with a radius of 150
million km (93 million mi)

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:39.472
encircling the Sun.

00:00:39.472 --> 00:00:41.541
A gigantic artificial world

00:00:41.541 --> 00:00:42.976
with its own gravity,

00:00:42.976 --> 00:00:45.278
ecosystem and atmosphere.

00:00:45.278 --> 00:00:48.281
Big enough for trillions
of humans to call home.

00:00:48.281 --> 00:00:52.419
You'd live on an enormous landmass
on the inner side of the ring.

00:00:52.419 --> 00:00:55.522
The outer shell would protect you
and all those trillions of people

00:00:55.522 --> 00:00:58.024
from the hazards of outer space.

00:01:00.493 --> 00:01:02.662
Problem is, assembling such a thing -

00:01:02.662 --> 00:01:04.631
suspended out in the Solar System -

00:01:04.631 --> 00:01:05.632
wouldn't be easy.

00:01:07.434 --> 00:01:09.469
You couldn't just pull the Earth apart

00:01:09.469 --> 00:01:10.804
and have an army of robots.

00:01:10.804 --> 00:01:13.506
reassemble it into a ringworld.

00:01:13.506 --> 00:01:15.775
Among the many problems you'd run into,

00:01:15.775 --> 00:01:18.812
your first would be finding the material.

00:01:18.812 --> 00:01:22.615
The International Space Station roaming
the Earth's lower orbit right now

00:01:22.615 --> 00:01:25.552
weighs about 420 tons.

00:01:25.552 --> 00:01:26.953
Something like a ringworld

00:01:26.953 --> 00:01:30.723
would tip the scales at
no less than a million tons.

00:01:30.723 --> 00:01:33.493
Where would we find all this material?

00:01:33.493 --> 00:01:34.894
I know some places.

00:01:34.894 --> 00:01:38.565
The Kuiper Belt beyond the orbit
of Neptune would do just fine.

00:01:38.565 --> 00:01:43.670
This ring of icy bodies stretches out for
almost 3 billion km (1.9 billion mi).

00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:45.638
Some astronomers think the Kuiper Belt

00:01:45.638 --> 00:01:47.974
would have enough material
for this project, but...

00:01:47.974 --> 00:01:50.076
it's hard to tell exactly how much we'd need

00:01:50.076 --> 00:01:52.345
to construct a thing like this.

00:01:52.345 --> 00:01:54.681
We might have to sacrifice all the planets,

00:01:54.681 --> 00:01:57.617
moons and asteroids in the Solar System.

00:01:57.617 --> 00:02:01.888
Our ringworld and the Sun
would be the only things left.

00:02:01.888 --> 00:02:05.792
If we could manage to gather and
transport all the material available,

00:02:05.792 --> 00:02:08.194
then construction would begin.

00:02:08.194 --> 00:02:10.163
It would take a lot of physical labor,

00:02:10.163 --> 00:02:13.533
an army of robots and maybe
hundreds of generations

00:02:13.533 --> 00:02:17.437
to realize that our structure
was not stable enough.

00:02:17.437 --> 00:02:20.106
Because the megastructure
would turn out so enormous,

00:02:20.106 --> 00:02:23.276
it would break any
known molecular bonds.

00:02:23.276 --> 00:02:27.180
We'd have to find a way to make use of
one of the fundamental forces of nature -

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:29.282
the strong nuclear force.

00:02:29.282 --> 00:02:31.885
Of all forces, it's the grippiest.

00:02:31.885 --> 00:02:34.787
It bonds material on the
scale of atomic nucleus

00:02:34.787 --> 00:02:36.923
so that nothing can break it apart.

00:02:36.923 --> 00:02:41.594
Or maybe we'd come up with a new
super-strong material altogether.

00:02:41.594 --> 00:02:43.263
But until we figure that out,

00:02:43.263 --> 00:02:46.432
every interstellar body passing
through our Solar System

00:02:46.432 --> 00:02:49.135
would be a threat to our megastructure.

00:02:49.135 --> 00:02:51.771
The next thing we'd have
to worry about is gravity.

00:02:51.771 --> 00:02:53.473
That part is pretty easy -

00:02:53.473 --> 00:02:57.744
we'd just have to spin the ringworld at
nearly 2,000,000 km/h (1,200,000 mi/h).

00:02:57.744 --> 00:02:59.679
I know, that's really fast.

00:02:59.679 --> 00:03:02.248
We'd have to build up the speed over time.

00:03:02.248 --> 00:03:05.952
Luckily, maintaining it in the
frictionless environment of space 

00:03:05.952 --> 00:03:07.854
wouldn't be too hard.

00:03:07.854 --> 00:03:10.790
Such rotation would generate centrifugal force,

00:03:10.790 --> 00:03:13.860
and that, in turn, would
create an artificial gravity

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:16.062
equal to the one we have here on Earth.

00:03:16.062 --> 00:03:18.965
With gravity solved, we'd
bring in the atmosphere

00:03:18.965 --> 00:03:21.267
and start populating the ringworld.

00:03:21.267 --> 00:03:24.804
For the inhabitants of the megastructure,
it would always be daytime.

00:03:24.804 --> 00:03:27.106
Unless we could create a day and night cycle

00:03:27.106 --> 00:03:29.676
with extra panels inside the ring.

00:03:29.676 --> 00:03:32.579
But for all the epicness of
the world we just created,

00:03:32.579 --> 00:03:34.380
it wouldn't be stable.

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:39.018
A single asteroid strike could cause
the structure to drift closer to the Sun.

00:03:39.018 --> 00:03:40.420
A hole punched through the ring

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:42.522
could let all our atmosphere out.

00:03:42.522 --> 00:03:44.591
And a massive solar storm?

00:03:44.591 --> 00:03:46.926
Don't even get me started on that one.

00:03:46.926 --> 00:03:48.494
One failure inside the ring

00:03:48.494 --> 00:03:52.398
could doom the entire structure
together with its inhabitants.

00:03:52.398 --> 00:03:55.134
It's just too risky to build it around the Sun.  

00:03:55.134 --> 00:03:56.469
We might have better luck

00:03:56.469 --> 00:04:00.306
with a huge ring space
station in the Earth's orbit.

00:04:00.306 --> 00:04:03.710
But that's a story for another WHAT IF.

